    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean by goblins? i'm just trying to fix the client site. is there a specific technical reason for the delay or is it just randomness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I was just using the goblin analogy to illustrate the idea that DNS changes can be a bit unpredictable and take time to propagate. But, of course, there are technical reasons behind the delay.\n\nThe main reason for the delay is that DNS changes are propagated through a hierarchical system of name servers, which can be slow to update. It's a bit like trying to get a message from the top of a tower to the bottom - it takes time for the message to travel down the chain. \n\nWhen you update an A record, the change needs to be propagated to all the recursive resolvers and authoritative name servers that serve the domain. This can take anywhere from 10 to 60 minutes, depending on the size of the network and the number of resolvers involved. It's a bit like waiting for a group of goblins to gather and agree on a single path to take - it's a slow process, but eventually, they'll get there.\n\nAnother factor that can contribute to the delay is the TTL (time to live) of the DNS record. If the TTL is set too low, the change might not propagate quickly enough. It's like trying to get a goblin to hurry up - they might not be in a hurry, but",
